1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,550
<font color="#FFFFFF">Reinforcement learning agents still learn far less
efficiently than humans,</font>

2
00:00:04,630 --> 00:00:08,680
<font color="#FFFFFF">who freely borrow strategies from many sources and
rearrange them at will.</font>

3
00:00:08,760 --> 00:00:12,810
<font color="#FFFFFF">This work, presented at NeurIPS 2023,</font>

4
00:00:12,890 --> 00:00:16,940
<font color="#FFFFFF">introduces Knowledge-Grounded Reinforcement Learning,</font>

5
00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:21,070
<font color="#FFFFFF">a paradigm that fuses multiple external knowledge
policies,</font>

6
00:00:21,150 --> 00:00:25,200
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a new actor architecture called the
Knowledge-Inclusive Attention Network,</font>

7
00:00:25,280 --> 00:00:29,330
<font color="#FFFFFF">or KIAN.</font>

8
00:00:29,410 --> 00:00:33,460
<font color="#FFFFFF">KIAN lets an agent add, remove, and recombine
knowledge policies without retraining,</font>

9
00:00:33,540 --> 00:00:37,590
<font color="#FFFFFF">and fixes an exploration pathology called entropy
imbalance,</font>

10
00:00:37,670 --> 00:00:41,720
<font color="#FFFFFF">delivering more sample-efficient and flexible
learning across grid-world and robotics tasks.</font>

11
00:00:42,100 --> 00:00:46,156
<font color="#FFFFFF">Reinforcement learning has succeeded across physics
and robotics,</font>

12
00:00:46,236 --> 00:00:52,011
<font color="#FFFFFF">yet agents still need enormous numbers of samples
to solve tasks that humans master quickly.</font>

13
00:00:52,091 --> 00:00:57,292
<font color="#FFFFFF">Part of the gap is that humans learn by observing
others and freely reuse, combine,</font>

14
00:00:57,372 --> 00:00:59,965
<font color="#FFFFFF">and swap the strategies they already know.</font>

15
00:01:00,045 --> 00:01:06,647
<font color="#FFFFFF">Earlier reinforcement learning methods did
incorporate external knowledge policies to improve efficiency,</font>

16
00:01:06,727 --> 00:01:12,437
<font color="#FFFFFF">but they made it hard to perform arbitrary
combinations and replacements of those policies.</font>

17
00:01:12,517 --> 00:01:16,510
<font color="#FFFFFF">That rigidity is exactly the property this paper
set out to fix.</font>

18
00:01:16,890 --> 00:01:19,973
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors summarize five properties of efficient,</font>

19
00:01:20,053 --> 00:01:25,244
<font color="#FFFFFF">flexible human learning: being
knowledge-acquirable, sample-efficient, generalizable,</font>

20
00:01:25,324 --> 00:01:27,166
<font color="#FFFFFF">compositional, and incremental.</font>

21
00:01:27,246 --> 00:01:33,739
<font color="#FFFFFF">Existing knowledge-guided reinforcement learning
methods satisfy some of these but stumble on flexibility.</font>

22
00:01:33,819 --> 00:01:38,886
<font color="#FFFFFF">When their fusion mechanism depends on the number
or ordering of external policies,</font>

23
00:01:38,966 --> 00:01:44,777
<font color="#FFFFFF">rearranging the knowledge set or swapping one
policy for another means rebuilding or retraining</font>

24
00:01:44,857 --> 00:01:46,328
<font color="#FFFFFF">large parts of the model.</font>

25
00:01:46,408 --> 00:01:52,715
<font color="#FFFFFF">The motivation here is to design an actor whose
structure lets knowledge policies be freely rearranged,</font>

26
00:01:52,795 --> 00:01:57,800
<font color="#FFFFFF">added, or replaced, so a single trained agent can
carry its skills into new tasks.</font>

27
00:01:58,180 --> 00:02:01,864
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper makes three main contributions.</font>

28
00:02:01,944 --> 00:02:05,628
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, it defines Knowledge-Grounded Reinforcement
Learning,</font>

29
00:02:05,708 --> 00:02:09,392
<font color="#FFFFFF">a paradigm that fuses an inner,</font>

30
00:02:09,472 --> 00:02:13,157
<font color="#FFFFFF">self-learned policy with multiple external
knowledge policies.</font>

31
00:02:13,237 --> 00:02:16,921
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, it introduces the Knowledge-Inclusive
Attention Network, KIAN,</font>

32
00:02:17,001 --> 00:02:20,685
<font color="#FFFFFF">whose embedding-based attention lets knowledge
policies be freely rearranged, added,</font>

33
00:02:20,765 --> 00:02:24,449
<font color="#FFFFFF">or replaced without touching the rest of the network.</font>

34
00:02:24,529 --> 00:02:28,213
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third,</font>

35
00:02:28,293 --> 00:02:31,977
<font color="#FFFFFF">it uncovers a problem called entropy imbalance that
arises when maximizing entropy for exploration,</font>

36
00:02:32,057 --> 00:02:35,742
<font color="#FFFFFF">proves when it happens, and proposes modified
policy distributions that fix it.</font>

37
00:02:35,822 --> 00:02:39,506
<font color="#FFFFFF">Together these give an agent that is efficient,
generalizable,</font>

38
00:02:39,586 --> 00:02:43,270
<font color="#FFFFFF">and truly modular in its use of knowledge.</font>

39
00:02:43,650 --> 00:02:48,753
<font color="#FFFFFF">KIAN, the Knowledge-Inclusive Attention Network, is
built from three components.</font>

40
00:02:48,833 --> 00:02:54,584
<font color="#FFFFFF">The inner actor is a normal learnable policy that
lets the agent develop its own strategy,</font>

41
00:02:54,664 --> 00:03:00,480
<font color="#FFFFFF">so even if every external policy is useless for a
task the agent can still find a solution.</font>

42
00:03:00,560 --> 00:03:03,525
<font color="#FFFFFF">Each knowledge policy, including the inner one,</font>

43
00:03:03,605 --> 00:03:06,958
<font color="#FFFFFF">is given a learnable embedding vector called its key,</font>

44
00:03:07,038 --> 00:03:11,429
<font color="#FFFFFF">which represents the whole policy independent of
any state or action.</font>

45
00:03:11,509 --> 00:03:17,519
<font color="#FFFFFF">A state-dependent query then produces a vector that
is compared to every key by a dot product,</font>

46
00:03:17,599 --> 00:03:21,147
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a softmax turns those scores into attention weights.</font>

47
00:03:21,227 --> 00:03:26,071
<font color="#FFFFFF">The agent fuses all policies as a weighted sum and
samples the final action.</font>

48
00:03:26,151 --> 00:03:31,189
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because each key is independent, the policies are
unordered, can be rearranged,</font>

49
00:03:31,269 --> 00:03:34,688
<font color="#FFFFFF">and any one can be replaced just by replacing its key,</font>

50
00:03:34,768 --> 00:03:37,020
<font color="#FFFFFF">without retraining the rest of KIAN.</font>

51
00:03:37,400 --> 00:03:41,221
<font color="#FFFFFF">The experiments span two families of environments.</font>

52
00:03:41,301 --> 00:03:45,122
<font color="#FFFFFF">MiniGrid provides discrete-action grid-world tasks
of increasing difficulty,</font>

53
00:03:45,202 --> 00:03:49,023
<font color="#FFFFFF">from empty rooms to door-key puzzles, dynamic
obstacles, lava crossings,</font>

54
00:03:49,103 --> 00:03:52,924
<font color="#FFFFFF">multi-room mazes, and key corridors.</font>

55
00:03:53,004 --> 00:03:56,825
<font color="#FFFFFF">OpenAI-Robotics provides continuous-control
manipulation tasks such as Push, Slide,</font>

56
00:03:56,905 --> 00:04:00,725
<font color="#FFFFFF">Pick-and-Place, and Reach.</font>

57
00:04:00,805 --> 00:04:04,626
<font color="#FFFFFF">Crucially,</font>

58
00:04:04,706 --> 00:04:08,527
<font color="#FFFFFF">every method starts from the same initial knowledge
set built from simple if-else programs</font>

59
00:04:08,607 --> 00:04:12,428
<font color="#FFFFFF">that are deliberately sub-optimal and cannot
complete any task on their own.</font>

60
00:04:12,508 --> 00:04:16,329
<font color="#FFFFFF">Each experiment is repeated with ten random seeds,</font>

61
00:04:16,409 --> 00:04:20,230
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the reported learning curves show ninety-five
percent confidence intervals.</font>

62
00:04:20,610 --> 00:04:22,560
<font color="#FFFFFF">Across both benchmark suites,</font>

63
00:04:22,640 --> 00:04:30,052
<font color="#FFFFFF">KIAN was the only method to succeed in every
environment when starting from sub-optimal external knowledge,</font>

64
00:04:30,132 --> 00:04:34,953
<font color="#FFFFFF">and its sample-efficiency advantage grew as tasks
became more complex.</font>

65
00:04:35,033 --> 00:04:36,914
<font color="#FFFFFF">In zero-shot generalization,</font>

66
00:04:36,994 --> 00:04:41,395
<font color="#FFFFFF">where a policy trained on one task is tested on a
different one,</font>

67
00:04:41,475 --> 00:04:47,976
<font color="#FFFFFF">KIAN outperformed all baselines in most transfers
and did so with noticeably smaller variance.</font>

68
00:04:48,056 --> 00:04:50,987
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the hardest simple-to-complex grid task,</font>

69
00:04:51,067 --> 00:04:56,798
<font color="#FFFFFF">KIAN reached a reward of about 0.93 while the
strongest baseline stalled near 0.53.</font>

70
00:04:56,878 --> 00:05:02,679
<font color="#FFFFFF">In continuous control, competing methods that
ignore the exploration issue collapse,</font>

71
00:05:02,759 --> 00:05:05,480
<font color="#FFFFFF">whereas KIAN keeps learning efficiently.</font>

72
00:05:05,860 --> 00:05:09,975
<font color="#FFFFFF">A key ablation isolates the paper's fix for entropy
imbalance.</font>

73
00:05:10,055 --> 00:05:13,155
<font color="#FFFFFF">When KIAN runs with the original policy fusion,</font>

74
00:05:13,235 --> 00:05:19,176
<font color="#FFFFFF">an agent maximizing entropy for exploration
collapses onto a single policy and struggles,</font>

75
00:05:19,256 --> 00:05:23,980
<font color="#FFFFFF">especially on demanding tasks like dynamic
obstacles, multi-room mazes,</font>

76
00:05:24,060 --> 00:05:26,348
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the robotic manipulation tasks.</font>

77
00:05:26,428 --> 00:05:33,790
<font color="#FFFFFF">Switching on the modified policy distributions
restores efficient exploration and recovers strong performance.</font>

78
00:05:33,870 --> 00:05:39,676
<font color="#FFFFFF">Separate compositional and incremental experiments
confirm the modular design pays off:</font>

79
00:05:39,756 --> 00:05:45,765
<font color="#FFFFFF">KIAN reuses its learned keys and inner policy to
acquire new tasks sequentially with fewer</font>

80
00:05:45,845 --> 00:05:48,810
<font color="#FFFFFF">samples than training each task from scratch.</font>

81
00:05:49,190 --> 00:05:51,544
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers capture the gains.</font>

82
00:05:51,624 --> 00:05:56,488
<font color="#FFFFFF">In zero-shot simple-to-complex transfer on the
Empty-Random grid,</font>

83
00:05:56,568 --> 00:06:04,398
<font color="#FFFFFF">KIAN scored about 0.93 at the sixteen-by-sixteen
size while the best competing method reached only 0.53.</font>

84
00:06:04,478 --> 00:06:09,493
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the Pick-and-Place task tested at a
ten-times-larger goal range,</font>

85
00:06:09,573 --> 00:06:13,905
<font color="#FFFFFF">KIAN reached 0.72 against 0.30 for the strongest baseline.</font>

86
00:06:13,985 --> 00:06:19,001
<font color="#FFFFFF">In complex-to-simple transfer from DoorKey
eight-by-eight to Reach,</font>

87
00:06:19,081 --> 00:06:25,161
<font color="#FFFFFF">KIAN achieved a perfect 1.0 versus 0.80 for the
reinforcement-learning baselines.</font>

88
00:06:25,241 --> 00:06:30,333
<font color="#FFFFFF">All of this is measured across two benchmark
suites, five baselines,</font>

89
00:06:30,413 --> 00:06:35,429
<font color="#FFFFFF">and ten random seeds with ninety-five percent
confidence intervals,</font>

90
00:06:35,509 --> 00:06:39,460
<font color="#FFFFFF">and KIAN is the only method that succeeds everywhere.</font>

91
00:06:39,840 --> 00:06:44,636
<font color="#FFFFFF">The lasting message is that treating each knowledge
policy as an independent,</font>

92
00:06:44,716 --> 00:06:50,081
<font color="#FFFFFF">attention-addressable key turns external knowledge
into truly modular building blocks.</font>

93
00:06:50,161 --> 00:06:51,538
<font color="#FFFFFF">An agent can add, drop,</font>

94
00:06:51,618 --> 00:06:55,780
<font color="#FFFFFF">or reorder its policies at any time without
retraining the network,</font>

95
00:06:55,860 --> 00:07:02,049
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the paper's fix for entropy imbalance keeps
exploration efficient when many policies are fused.</font>

96
00:07:02,129 --> 00:07:07,368
<font color="#FFFFFF">The result is a reinforcement learning actor that
learns faster, generalizes better,</font>

97
00:07:07,448 --> 00:07:08,571
<font color="#FFFFFF">and stays flexible,</font>

98
00:07:08,651 --> 00:07:13,700
<font color="#FFFFFF">moving agents a step closer to the efficiency and
adaptability of human learning.</font>
